  "final_report": "[stop_reason: hop_cap_reached | hops used: 4/4]\n\nAnalyst summary:\n**Investigation focus:**  \nA disruption at **Formosa Petrochemical** (first reported\u202f\u2248\u202f2019\u201104\u201107) \u2013 we needed to identify downstream customers or other firms that could be structurally at risk and then gather evidence from SEC filings or corpus mentions.\n\n### 1. Structural\u2010risk map (downstream, up to two tiers)\n\nThe **traverse_supply_graph** call returned a list of 98 \u201cenriched\u201d companies reachable within two tiers. The most relevant tier\u20111 downstream customers (direct users of Formosa Petrochemical\u2019s products) are:\n\n| Tier | Company (sector) | Primary product that could depend on Formosa Petrochemical |\n|------|------------------|------------------------------------------------------------|\n| 1    | **JSR** (Chemicals, Japan) \u2013 synthetic rubber (SBR/BR) |\n| 1    | **Sumitomo Chemical** (Chemicals, Japan) \u2013 engineering plastics & Li\u2011ion battery materials |\n| 1    | **Glencore** (Metals\u202f&\u202fMining, UK) \u2013 cobalt (as a by\u2011product of copper) |\n| 1    | **Formosa Plastics** (Chemicals, Taiwan) \u2013 PVC resin and epichlorohydrin |\n| 1    | **Zeon** (Chemicals, Japan) \u2013 specialty synthetic rubber (HNBR/polybutadiene) |\n| 1    | **PetroChina** (Oil\u202f&\u202fGas Producers, China) \u2013 benzene & aniline petrochemicals |\n| 1    | **Formosa Chemicals & Fibre** (Chemicals, Taiwan) \u2013 PTA/styrene & synthetic fibers |\n| 1    | **DL Holdings Co. Ltd.** (Construction Materials, South Korea) \u2013 polyethylene & polybutene (via DL Chemical) |\n| 1    | **Taita Chemical** (Chemicals, Taiwan) \u2013 polystyrene\u2011type resins (ABS/GPPS/HIPS/EPS) |\n| 1    | **Jiangsu Lopal Tech** (Chemicals, China) \u2013 automotive lubricants / DEF & LFP cathode materials |\n| \u2026 (additional tier\u20111 firms listed in the traversal output) |\n\nTier\u20112 companies are one step further removed (customers of the tier\u20111 firms). The list is very long (dozens of automakers, battery makers, steel producers, etc.). Because we have only four tool calls total and already used three (traversal + two get_supplier_info attempts), we cannot obtain additional evidence for most of these.\n\n### 2. Evidence gathered\n\n| Company | Source(s) consulted | What the source says / shows |\n|---------|--------------------|--------------------------------|\n| **JSR** | `get_supplier_info` (no filing found) | No SEC\u2011type filing on record \u2013 we could not locate any self\u2011disclosed information linking JSR to Formosa Petrochemical. |\n| **Formosa Plastics** | `get_supplier_info` (no filing found) | No filing on record for this entity in the database. |\n| **All other tier\u20111 & tier\u20112 firms** | Not directly queried (budget exhausted). The traversal output itself is algorithmic evidence that these companies are *structurally linked* (i.e., they appear as downstream customers or alternative suppliers based on supply\u2011chain graph data). No company\u2011specific filing excerpts were retrieved. |\n| **General \u201cFormosa Petrochemical disruption\u201d** | `search_corpus` (10 results) \u2013 the returned hits relate to unrelated incidents at other firms (Eni, BP, etc.) and do not mention Formosa Petrochemical or its downstream customers. No direct corpus evidence of a 2019\u201104\u201107 disruption was found. |\n\n### 3. What could NOT be confirmed\n\n* **Direct documentary confirmation** that any specific downstream customer experienced material impact from the Formosa Petrochemical event.  \n  * We attempted to retrieve filings for JSR and Formosa Plastics \u2013 none existed in the searchable repository.  \n* **Corpus mentions** of the disruption itself (e.g., news\u2011style filing disclosures, risk factor statements) \u2013 the search returned only unrelated environmental or accident disclosures from other companies; no mention of a 2019\u201104\u201107 Formosa Petrochemical incident was found.  \n\n### 4. Assessment of impact\n\n* The **graph traversal** shows that a large number of downstream firms (especially chemical producers, plastics makers, and automotive\u2011related material suppliers) are *potentially* exposed to supply interruptions from Formosa Petrochemical.  \n* However, without concrete filing excerpts or corpus citations linking these firms to the specific 2019 disruption, we cannot verify whether they actually reported operational or financial effects. The lack of filings for JSR and Formosa Plastics \u2013 two of the most directly linked tier\u20111 customers \u2013 suggests either (a) the companies do not file with the SEC (they are non\u2011U.S. entities), or (b) the database does not contain their disclosures, limiting our ability to confirm impact.\n\n### 5. Conclusion\n\n- **Structurally at risk**: dozens of firms across chemicals, plastics, automotive, battery and steel sectors (see tier\u20111 list above).  \n- **Evidence**: The only concrete evidence we have is the algorithmic supply\u2011chain linkage from the traversal; no SEC filing or corpus mention directly confirms that any of these companies suffered a disruption due to Formosa Petrochemical\u2019s 2019 event.  \n- **Unconfirmed**: Specific downstream impact on JSR, Formosa Plastics, and all other listed firms remains unverified because relevant filings were not found and the corpus search returned no relevant disclosures.\n\n**Result:** The investigation identifies many potentially affected companies but lacks documentary confirmation of actual disruption effects. Further research would require additional tool calls (e.g., targeted `get_supplier_info` for each downstream firm that files with the SEC, or broader news\u2011media searches) to move from structural risk to confirmed impact.\n\nCOVERAGE GAPS (97) -- reported explicitly, not omitted:\n  - jsr: no evidence gathered\n  - sumitomo chemical: no evidence gathered\n